# The response headers of https://mtg-grimoire.app — Cloudflare's `_headers` format, read by
# Workers static assets from the root of the uploaded directory. The web build copies this file
# into `dist-web/` and into no other bundle (`vite.mobile.config.ts`, mode `web`); it is parsed at
# deploy and is not itself served. `app-worker/src/headers.ts` reads it the same way for
# `npm run web:preview`, and `app-worker/src/hosting.test.ts` holds every line below to the
# engine's own constants.
#
# Every matching rule applies, in order, and a header two rules both set is JOINED WITH A COMMA.
# So a narrower rule that replaces a value detaches the wider one first: `! Name`.
#
# These reach static-asset responses only. What the Worker's script answers itself — a 404 — carries
# its own headers, set in `src/index.ts`.

# --- Everything ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# ONE policy on every response, the scripts included, and that is deliberate: a dedicated Worker
# and a service worker are each governed by the policy on THEIR OWN script's response, not the
# page's (measured in Chrome 154 — README, "The policy"). The engine is compiled inside the
# database Worker, so `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` has to arrive on that chunk; the launch's downloads
# leave from it, so `connect-src` does too. Sending the page one policy and the scripts another
# would be two lists to keep equal.
#
#   script-src   `'wasm-unsafe-eval'` is what compiling a WebAssembly module asks for and all it
#                grants. Never `'unsafe-eval'`, which grants `eval` with it.
#   style-src    `'self'`, the desktop's rule: no runtime <style>. `src/CLAUDE.md` has what that
#                forbids, and the same components run here.
#   connect-src  EXACTLY the hosts the engine asks, each from a constant in `crates/grimoire-core`:
#                  api.scryfall.com             launch.rs      SCRYFALL_API
#                  data.scryfall.io             the bulk files — Scryfall's descriptor names the
#                                               address, so this is their host and not our constant
#                  cards.scryfall.io            image_uri.rs   IMAGE_HOST
#                  json.commanderspellbook.com  combos.rs      FEED_URL
#                  api.cardkingdom.com          marketplace_feed.rs, Card Kingdom's pricelist
#                NOT manapool.com: it sends no Access-Control-Allow-Origin, so a page cannot read
#                it whatever this says. NOT the relay: sync in a browser is phase 6, which adds the
#                host here in the change that adds this origin to the relay's CORS allow-list.
#   img-src      This origin and `data:`, and NOT cards.scryfall.io although every card picture
#                comes from there. A picture is asked of this origin (`/mtgimg/…`) and answered by
#                the service worker — which must hand back a response REBUILT FROM THE BYTES.
#                Chrome checks the address of the response a service worker returns as well as
#                the address the page asked: Scryfall's response passed along as it came, or out
#                of Cache Storage, is refused by this line (measured, Chrome 154 — README). The
#                host is in `connect-src`, which is what the service worker's own `fetch` needs.
#   frame-ancestors, object-src, frame-src, base-uri, form-action — nothing embeds this app, and
#                it embeds, rebases and posts nothing.
#
# `Referrer-Policy`: the origin and never a path. Scryfall asks an app to say who it is, and a
# browser gives a page no User-Agent to say it with; what a shop behind an `Open on …` link learns
# is the app's name and not which deck was open.
#
# `Cache-Control: no-cache` is the default for every address: the document (at `/` and at every
# route the single-page fallback answers), the manifest, the favicon and `icons/*.png` — each is
# revalidated before use, so a deploy is seen on the next load. The manifest's icons are in that
# list on purpose: their names carry no hash, so a year for them would be a year an installed
# app kept an old icon, and a revalidation is a 304. The two trees below are the exception.
#
# No `Strict-Transport-Security`: the whole `.app` top-level domain is on the browsers' HSTS
# preload list. No `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy` or `-Embedder-Policy`: the OPFS pool needs neither
# (the August spike ran it both ways), and `require-corp` would refuse every card picture.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; worker-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; style-src-attr 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self' data:; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://api.scryfall.com https://data.scryfall.io https://cards.scryfall.io https://json.commanderspellbook.com https://api.cardkingdom.com; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Cache-Control: no-cache

# --- Content-addressed: kept for a year, never asked about again ---------------------------------
#
# Vite names every file under `assets/` by a hash of its bytes, and the engine's two files sit in
# a directory named by a hash of theirs (`src/lib/core/web/assets.ts`, `buildIdOf`). A changed
# byte is a new address, so nothing at an old address is ever stale.
/assets/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/wasm/*
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# --- The service worker's script ----------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE FILE ARRIVES IN STEP 5.3; until then this rule matches nothing, and a request for `/sw.js`
# is the Worker's 404. It is written now because it is the one address whose caching decides
# whether a deploy can ever be seen: a service worker script kept by the HTTP cache is an update
# no reader is offered. The value is the default's, restated so that loosening `/*` cannot take
# this with it. It must stay at the root and unhashed — a service worker controls its own path
# and below — so it must never be given the year the two trees above have.
/sw.js
  ! Cache-Control
  Cache-Control: no-cache
